#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Wait for the reviewer-gate verdict on a PR. Always emits a final result on
# stdout — an "Approval ..."/"Review ..." line, or a JSON object with --json —
# so callers can route approved vs changes-requested vs timeout vs proceeded
# deterministically (vstack#538, vstack#642).
#
# Two wait modes (--mode, default approval):
#
# approval — GitHub-native approval detection ONLY, either signal:
#   - reviewDecision == "APPROVED" (branch-protection aggregate), or
#   - when reviewDecision is empty/null (no required-review protection):
#     at least one reviewer whose LATEST review is APPROVED and no reviewer
#     whose latest review is CHANGES_REQUESTED.
# Any reviewer counts — human or bot — as long as it posts a formal GitHub
# review. Emoji reactions, sticky comments, and checklist prose are never
# parsed. A reviewDecision of REVIEW_REQUIRED never falls back to
# latestReviews: branch protection is still waiting on required approvals.
#
# review — for repos whose reviewers post COMMENTED reviews and never approve
# (vstack#642). Succeeds (status "reviewed") when ALL of:
#   - review evidence for the CURRENT head commit, either:
#       (a) a submitted review pinned to the head (REST commit_id ==
#           headRefOid), not DISMISSED/PENDING, and not the PR author's own —
#           any state counts; an approval is also a review; or
#       (b) PR_REVIEW_CHECK is non-empty and the current head carries a
#           successful signal of exactly that name on EITHER evidence
#           surface (vstack#654, vstack#681): the newest check-run with
#           that name concluding "success", or — consulted only when no
#           check-run matched — the head's combined commit status carrying
#           a context of that name at state "success". For review bots
#           that submit a review object only when they have findings,
#           whose clean re-analyses would otherwise never satisfy the
#           gate; some such bots publish commit STATUSES (statuses API),
#           not check-runs, so both surfaces are matched;
#   - no non-author reviewer's latest submitted review stands at
#     CHANGES_REQUESTED (dismissal or a newer review clears it);
#   - zero unresolved review threads.
# The head SHA is re-read every poll, so a force-push resets the wait: a
# review of a superseded commit never satisfies the gate, and check-runs and
# commit statuses are only ever queried on the current head.
#
# The poller also returns early (status "comments") when unresolved review
# threads exist without the mode's verdict, so callers can triage new
# comments instead of idling out the full timeout.
#
# Transient GitHub API failures (vstack#748): inside the poll loop, an HTTP
# 5xx, HTTP 429 rate limit, timeout, or transport error from any GitHub
# query is RETRYABLE — the
# poller logs it to stderr, doubles the poll interval for the next attempt
# (capped at 8x), and keeps waiting inside the existing max_wait budget. A
# fully successful poll resets the backoff. Only when the budget expires on
# a still-failing call does the last error become terminal, reported with a
# transient_api_errors count. Non-transient failures (HTTP 404, 401/403,
# malformed arguments) stay immediately terminal.
#
# Nudging (both modes): when the mode's signal has not arrived within
# PR_REVIEW_NUDGE_SECS (default 600; 0 disables) of the wait starting or of
# the head last changing — a push restarts the clock — the poller nudges the
# reviewer ONCE per head SHA, then keeps waiting inside the existing max_wait
# bound. The nudge is the user-configured PR_REVIEW_NUDGE comment body posted
# on the PR (no bot names are baked in); when it is empty the fallback is a
# GitHub-native re-review request to the PR's requested and past reviewers,
# or silence when there is nobody to re-request. Both keys read with the
# orch-env precedence (process env > vstack.settings.toml > default).
#
# Reviewer-down flexibility (PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT, default "block"): when the
# gate signal never arrives before the deadline — the reviewer posted nothing,
# e.g. its credits are exhausted — "block" reports "timeout" (exit 1) as
# before, and the caller prompts or keeps waiting. "proceed" instead reports a
# distinct "proceeded" (exit 0), but ONLY on genuine reviewer silence: zero
# unresolved threads (an open thread returns "comments" before the deadline),
# no reviewer engagement of any kind, AND a head that did not change during
# this wait. "No engagement" means: no review pinned to the current head, and
# (review mode) no named trusted check/status present at head in any state — a
# pending or failed reviewer check counts as engagement, not silence — and
# (approval mode) no active COMMENTED review either. A force-push that moved the
# head mid-wait falls back to "timeout" (the caller re-waits on the now-stable
# head), and the head is re-confirmed once more at the deadline itself — closing
# the last-poll -> emit window — so neither the proceed nor its outage marker can
# land on a commit that was superseded after the final poll. Proceed thus never
# bypasses a live gate and never attests a stale head — only routes around a
# genuinely dead reviewer.
# The caller records "proceeded" as a reviewer-down override; CI and the
# comment-hygiene gate still apply. The --on-timeout flag overrides the setting;
# both read with orch-env precedence (process env > vstack.settings.toml >
# default).
#
# Reviewer-outage attestation (PR_REVIEW_OUTAGE_CONTEXT, default empty; review
# mode only): on a repo whose CI/branch-protection gate independently blocks
# merges on review evidence, the orch-side "proceeded" alone does not unblock
# the merge — that gate runs on events, not on orch's wait deadline. When this
# names a commit-status context, a review-mode proceed posts that context as
# `success` on the proceeded head, giving such a gate a signal to accept (as an
# extra review-evidence surface) and its status re-fire bridge an event to re-run
# on. Approval mode never posts it (its silence signal excludes reviewer checks,
# and a status cannot satisfy native required-approvals).
# It is a branch-protection relaxation bounded by the same genuine-silence
# predicate as the proceed itself and by bot-only status trust; match-by-context,
# so name it only where every status publisher is trusted. Empty = orch-side
# only (no marker; the repo-side gate is untouched). See orch DEVELOPMENT.md
# "Reviewer-outage recognition" for the repo-side gate/bridge pattern.
#
# Usage: approval-wait <PR#> [poll_interval] [max_wait] [--json] [--mode approval|review] [--on-timeout block|proceed]
#        approval-wait --resolve-mode
#   poll_interval default 15s, max_wait default 900s (15 min).
#
# --resolve-mode prints the project's effective reviewer-gate mode and exits:
# PR_REVIEW_GATE (approval|review|off) when set; otherwise the legacy
# PR_APPROVAL_GATE maps on → approval and off → off; both unset defaults to
# approval. Same env > vstack.settings.toml > default precedence as orch-env.
# This is the single implementation of the back-compat derivation — callers
# read the mode through it instead of re-deriving the chain.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0 - approved (approval mode) / reviewed (review mode) / proceeded
#       (deadline reached with zero unresolved threads under
#       PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT=proceed)
#   1 - changes requested, unresolved comments pending, timeout, or a
#       non-auth error
#   3 - GitHub auth/CLI error (matches ci-wait)
#
# JSON output (always when --json):
#   {
#     "status": "approved" | "reviewed" | "changes_requested" | "comments" | "timeout" | "proceeded" | "error",
#     "review_decision": <string>,  # raw reviewDecision ("" when unset)
#     "approvals": <int>,           # reviewers whose latest review is APPROVED
#     "changes_requested": <int>,   # reviewers whose latest review is CHANGES_REQUESTED
#     "unresolved_count": <int>,    # unresolved review threads at emit time
#     "elapsed_seconds": <int>,
#     "mode": <string>,             # review mode only
#     "head_sha": <string>,         # review mode only: head at last poll
#     "reviews_at_head": <int>,     # review mode only: non-author reviews of head
#     "review_evidence": <string>,  # review mode only, when the gate passes:
#                                   # "review" (review object) or "check"
#                                   # (PR_REVIEW_CHECK success on either surface)
#     "review_evidence_surface": <string>,  # review mode only, alongside
#                                   # review_evidence "check": "check_run" or
#                                   # "status" — the API surface that matched
#     "transient_api_errors": <int>,  # only when > 0: transient GitHub API
#                                   # failures absorbed by retrying (vstack#748)
#     "outage_marker": <string>,    # review-mode proceed with
#                                   # PR_REVIEW_OUTAGE_CONTEXT set: "posted" or
#                                   # "failed" (the status POST was rejected —
#                                   # the repo-side CI gate got no signal and
#                                   # cannot recover; retry or stop, do not idle
#                                   # ci-wait). Absent when not attempted.
#     "error": <string>             # only when status == "error"
#   }
#
# Auth resolution ladder (see vstack#19):
#   1. Validate the currently selected GH_TOKEN/GITHUB_TOKEN once, if present.
#   2. If that env token fails, try keyring auth once with env tokens removed.
#   3. If keyring does not recover, unset the stale env token and load a
#      GH_BOT_TOKEN candidate from project config/secrets.
#   4. Validate the loaded bot token once, or keyring once when no env token
#      was present at startup.
#   5. If no auth path works after that, exit 3 with a clear diagnostic.
#      Exit 3 matches ci-wait's auth-error exit so callers can treat the two
#      consistently.
set -euo pipefail

SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
PROJECT_ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || pwd)"

# shellcheck source=lib/gh-auth.sh
source "$SCRIPT_DIR/lib/gh-auth.sh"

# --- arg parse (before auth so error paths can honor --json) ---
JSON_OUTPUT=false
MODE="approval"
RESOLVE_MODE=false
ON_TIMEOUT_FLAG=""
POSITIONAL=()
while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
  case "$1" in
    --json) JSON_OUTPUT=true; shift ;;
    --resolve-mode) RESOLVE_MODE=true; shift ;;
    --mode)
      MODE="${2:?approval-wait: --mode requires a value: approval or review}"
      shift 2
      ;;
    --on-timeout)
      ON_TIMEOUT_FLAG="${2:?approval-wait: --on-timeout requires a value: block or proceed}"
      shift 2
      ;;
    *) POSITIONAL+=("$1"); shift ;;
  esac
done

# --resolve-mode: print the effective reviewer-gate mode and exit. Needs no
# auth or PR argument — it only reads project configuration, with the same
# precedence as orch-env (process env > vstack.settings.toml > default).
if $RESOLVE_MODE; then
  # shellcheck source=lib/vstack-env.sh
  source "$SCRIPT_DIR/lib/vstack-env.sh"
  vstack_load_project_env "$PROJECT_ROOT"
  mode="${PR_REVIEW_GATE:-}"
  case "$mode" in
    approval|review|off) ;;
    "")
      # Legacy PR_APPROVAL_GATE derivation: off → off, anything else (or
      # unset) → approval — the pre-#642 binary gate semantics.
      if [[ "${PR_APPROVAL_GATE:-}" == "off" ]]; then
        mode="off"
      else
        mode="approval"
      fi
      ;;
    *)
      echo "approval-wait: unrecognized PR_REVIEW_GATE value '$mode'; using approval" >&2
      mode="approval"
      ;;
  esac
  printf '%s\n' "$mode"
  exit 0
fi

case "$MODE" in
  approval|review) ;;
  *)
    echo "approval-wait: invalid --mode '$MODE' (expected approval or review)" >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac

PR_NUM="${POSITIONAL[0]:?Usage: approval-wait <PR#> [poll_interval] [max_wait] [--json] [--mode approval|review]}"
POLL_INTERVAL="${POSITIONAL[1]:-15}"
MAX_WAIT="${POSITIONAL[2]:-900}"
START_TIME="$(date +%s)"
elapsed=0

# Nudge configuration (vstack#642), read through orch-env so the same
# process env > vstack.settings.toml > default precedence applies without
# sourcing project env files into this process. The numeric default guard in
# orch-env keeps NUDGE_SECS usable; 0 disables nudging.
NUDGE_SECS="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/orch-env" PR_REVIEW_NUDGE_SECS 600)"
NUDGE_BODY="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/orch-env" PR_REVIEW_NUDGE "")"

# Review-check evidence (vstack#654, vstack#681): PR_REVIEW_CHECK names the
# check the repo's review bot publishes on analyzed heads; review mode accepts
# a successful signal of that name on the current head as an alternative to a
# review object, on EITHER surface — a check-run concluding "success"
# (check-runs API), or a commit status context at state "success" (combined
# status API), because some review bots publish statuses rather than
# check-runs. SECURITY: both surfaces are matched by NAME/context only, and
# any GitHub App, Actions workflow, or token holder with statuses scope can
# publish under any name — the setting must name a signal produced by the
# TRUSTED review bot. Like PR_REVIEW_NUDGE, the trust model is user
# configuration; no bot names are baked in, and the matched check-run's app
# slug (or the matched status's creator login) is reported (not filtered on)
# so operators can audit who satisfied the gate. Empty disables check
# evidence entirely.
REVIEW_CHECK_NAME="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/orch-env" PR_REVIEW_CHECK "")"

# Reviewer-down flexibility (PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT, default "block"): controls
# the deadline verdict when the gate signal never arrives. "block" reports
# "timeout" (exit 1) as before — the caller prompts or keeps waiting.
# "proceed" reports a distinct "proceeded" (exit 0) INSTEAD, but only when zero
# threads are unresolved, so a credit-exhausted reviewer that posted nothing
# never blocks the fleet. An open thread can never reach this path: an
# unresolved thread exits the poll loop as "comments" first, so a real comment
# is never bypassed. The --on-timeout flag overrides the setting for explicit
# callers and tests; same env > vstack.settings.toml > default precedence.
ON_TIMEOUT="${ON_TIMEOUT_FLAG:-$("$SCRIPT_DIR/orch-env" PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT block)}"
case "$ON_TIMEOUT" in
  block|proceed) ;;
  *)
    echo "approval-wait: unrecognized PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT value '$ON_TIMEOUT'; using block" >&2
    ON_TIMEOUT="block"
    ;;
esac

# Reviewer-outage attestation (PR_REVIEW_OUTAGE_CONTEXT, default empty; review
# mode only — see post_outage_marker). When a proceed degrade fires (reviewer
# genuinely down), a repo whose CI gate blocks merges on review evidence needs
# a signal it can accept — CI runs on events,
# not on a wait deadline, so it has no notion of "orch hit the deadline". If
# this names a commit-status context, orch posts that context as `success` on
# the proceeded head (below): the repo's CI gate can add it as an accepted
# review-evidence surface, and its status re-fire bridge re-runs the gate on the
# status event, turning the required check green. SECURITY: this is a
# branch-protection relaxation — the status lets a merge proceed with no review.
# It is bounded by the same hardened predicate that gates the proceed itself
# (genuine silence only) and by the bot-only trust of the status API, and it is
# match-by-context, so name it only on repos where every status publisher is
# trusted (the same trust model as PR_REVIEW_CHECK). Empty = post nothing (orch
# proceeds but the repo-side CI gate is unaffected — orch-side-only opt-in).
OUTAGE_CONTEXT="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/orch-env" PR_REVIEW_OUTAGE_CONTEXT "")"

update_elapsed() {
  local now
  now="$(date +%s)"
  elapsed=$((now - START_TIME))
}

# Last review snapshot seen by the poll loop, so timeout/error results can
# report the review state that was still outstanding.
last_review_decision=""
last_approvals=0
last_changes=0
last_unresolved=0
last_head_sha=""
last_reviews_at_head=0
# Approval mode only: non-author, non-dismissed latest reviews of ANY state
# (COMMENTED included). Distinguishes "a reviewer engaged but did not approve"
# from "no reviewer showed at all", so the PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT=proceed degrade
# fires only on genuine reviewer silence — never past an active COMMENTED
# review. Review mode leaves this 0 and relies on its at-head evidence instead.
last_reviews_present=0
last_review_evidence=""
last_check_surface=""
last_check_app=""
last_check_creator=""
# Outage-marker outcome, reported in the JSON so a caller can distinguish a
# proceed whose marker landed (repo-side gate will re-fire green) from one whose
# marker POST failed (gate stays red, cannot recover on its own). "" = not
# attempted (no context / approval mode); "posted"; "failed".
last_outage_marker=""

# Transient-failure bookkeeping (vstack#748): every gh call in the poll loop
# writes stderr to GH_ERR_FILE so a failure can be classified before it is
# allowed to terminate the wait. retry_interval doubles per consecutive
# transient failure (capped) and resets after any fully successful poll.
transient_api_errors=0
GH_ERR_FILE="$(mktemp)"
trap 'rm -f "$GH_ERR_FILE"' EXIT
retry_interval="$POLL_INTERVAL"
RETRY_INTERVAL_CAP=$((POLL_INTERVAL * 8))

# Nudge bookkeeping: the clock restarts when the head changes, and each head
# SHA is nudged at most once ("__none__" so an unknown head still gets one).
last_reviewer_logins=""
nudge_clock="$START_TIME"
prev_head_sha=""
last_nudged_sha="__none__"
# Set true if the head moves after the first poll. A force-push near MAX_WAIT
# would otherwise let the very next poll see no evidence on the new commit and
# emit "proceeded" seconds later — skipping a reviewer who has not yet seen the
# push. When the head changed during this wait, the deadline falls back to
# "timeout" (the caller re-waits on the now-stable head, whose own wait can
# proceed if the reviewer stays genuinely silent). Elapsed is measured from the
# original START_TIME, so this per-head guard is what makes proceed head-fair.
head_changed_during_wait=false

# Emit the final machine-readable result on stdout. Every exit path routes
# through here (or emit_error) so the script can never finish silently.
#   status: approved | reviewed | changes_requested | comments | timeout
#         | proceeded | error
emit_result() {
  local status="$1" error_msg="${2:-}" mode_fields
  update_elapsed
  if $JSON_OUTPUT; then
    # Review-mode fields are additive only, so approval-mode JSON stays
    # byte-compatible with the pre-#642 contract.
    mode_fields='{}'
    if [[ "$MODE" == "review" ]]; then
      # review_evidence appears only when the gate passed — "review" for a
      # review object at head, "check" for PR_REVIEW_CHECK success (vstack#654)
      # on either surface; review_evidence_surface then pins which API surface
      # matched — "check_run" or "status" (vstack#681).
      mode_fields=$(jq -n \
        --arg mode "$MODE" \
        --arg head "$last_head_sha" \
        --argjson at_head "$last_reviews_at_head" \
        --arg evidence "$last_review_evidence" \
        --arg surface "$last_check_surface" \
        '{mode: $mode, head_sha: $head, reviews_at_head: $at_head}
         + (if $evidence == "" then {} else {review_evidence: $evidence} end)
         + (if $evidence == "check" and $surface != ""
            then {review_evidence_surface: $surface} else {} end)')
    fi
    jq -n \
      --arg s "$status" \
      --arg decision "$last_review_decision" \
      --argjson approvals "$last_approvals" \
      --argjson changes "$last_changes" \
      --argjson unresolved "$last_unresolved" \
      --argjson elapsed "$elapsed" \
      --argjson mode_fields "$mode_fields" \
      --argjson transient "$transient_api_errors" \
      --arg marker "$last_outage_marker" \
      --arg error "$error_msg" \
      '{
        status: $s,
        review_decision: $decision,
        approvals: $approvals,
        changes_requested: $changes,
        unresolved_count: $unresolved,
        elapsed_seconds: $elapsed
      } + $mode_fields
        + (if $transient > 0 then {transient_api_errors: $transient} else {} end)
        + (if $marker == "" then {} else {outage_marker: $marker} end)
        + (if $error == "" then {} else {error: $error} end)'
    return 0
  fi
  case "$status" in
    approved)
      echo "Approval: approved (decision: ${last_review_decision:-none}, approvals: $last_approvals, unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      ;;
    reviewed)
      if [[ "$last_review_evidence" == "check" && "$last_check_surface" == "status" ]]; then
        echo "Review: reviewed via status '$REVIEW_CHECK_NAME'${last_check_creator:+ (creator: $last_check_creator)} (head: ${last_head_sha:-unknown}, unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      elif [[ "$last_review_evidence" == "check" ]]; then
        echo "Review: reviewed via check-run '$REVIEW_CHECK_NAME'${last_check_app:+ (app: $last_check_app)} (head: ${last_head_sha:-unknown}, unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      else
        echo "Review: reviewed (head: ${last_head_sha:-unknown}, reviews at head: $last_reviews_at_head, unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      fi
      ;;
    proceeded)
      if [[ "$MODE" == "review" ]]; then
        echo "Review: no reviewer evidence after ${elapsed}s — proceeding per PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT=proceed (head: ${last_head_sha:-unknown}, unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      else
        echo "Approval: no approval verdict after ${elapsed}s — proceeding per PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT=proceed (decision: ${last_review_decision:-none}, unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      fi
      ;;
    changes_requested)
      if [[ "$MODE" == "review" ]]; then
        echo "Review: changes requested ($last_changes reviewer(s), unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      else
        echo "Approval: changes requested ($last_changes reviewer(s), unresolved threads: $last_unresolved)"
      fi
      ;;
    comments)
      if [[ "$MODE" == "review" ]]; then
        echo "Review: $last_unresolved unresolved review thread(s) pending triage"
      else
        echo "Approval: $last_unresolved unresolved review thread(s) pending triage, no approval verdict yet"
      fi
      ;;
    timeout)
      if [[ "$MODE" == "review" ]]; then
        echo "Review timeout after ${elapsed}s (head: ${last_head_sha:-unknown}, reviews at head: $last_reviews_at_head)"
      else
        echo "Approval timeout after ${elapsed}s (decision: ${last_review_decision:-none}, approvals: $last_approvals)"
      fi
      ;;
    error)
      if [[ "$MODE" == "review" ]]; then
        echo "Review error: $error_msg"
      else
        echo "Approval error: $error_msg"
      fi
      ;;
  esac
}

emit_error() {
  emit_result "error" "$1"
}

if ! command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  emit_error "gh CLI not found on PATH"
  echo "approval-wait: gh CLI not found on PATH" >&2
  exit 3
fi

AUTH_READY=false
KEYRING_PROBED=false

if [[ -n "${GH_TOKEN:-}${GITHUB_TOKEN:-}" ]]; then
  auth_status=0
  if orch_github_auth_status; then
    AUTH_READY=true
  else
    auth_status=$?
    if [[ "$auth_status" -ne 124 && "${VSTACK_GITHUB_SELECTED_TOKEN_SOURCE:-}" != "GH_BOT_TOKEN" ]]; then
      KEYRING_PROBED=true
      if orch_github_keyring_auth_status; then
        echo "Warning: GH_TOKEN/GITHUB_TOKEN failed gh auth; unsetting them and using gh keyring auth." >&2
        unset GH_TOKEN GITHUB_TOKEN
        export VSTACK_GITHUB_AUTH_FALLBACK="keyring"
        AUTH_READY=true
      else
        unset GH_TOKEN GITHUB_TOKEN
      fi
    else
      unset GH_TOKEN GITHUB_TOKEN
    fi
  fi
fi

if [[ "$AUTH_READY" != "true" && -z "${GH_TOKEN:-}${GITHUB_TOKEN:-}" ]]; then
  orch_load_env_bot_token "$PROJECT_ROOT" || true
fi

if [[ "$AUTH_READY" != "true" ]]; then
  if [[ -n "${GH_TOKEN:-}${GITHUB_TOKEN:-}" ]]; then
    if orch_github_auth_status; then
      AUTH_READY=true
    fi
  elif [[ "$KEYRING_PROBED" != "true" ]]; then
    KEYRING_PROBED=true
    if orch_github_auth_status; then
      export VSTACK_GITHUB_AUTH_FALLBACK="keyring"
      AUTH_READY=true
    fi
  fi
fi

if [[ "$AUTH_READY" != "true" ]]; then
  emit_error "no working GitHub auth path"
  {
    echo "approval-wait: no working GitHub auth path."
    echo "Tried: env GH_TOKEN/GITHUB_TOKEN, gh keyring, project GH_BOT_TOKEN."
    echo "Run: gh auth login   (or set a valid GH_BOT_TOKEN in .env.local)."
  } >&2
  exit 3
fi

# --- Resolve repo explicitly so gh calls don't depend on local auth/inference ---
REPO=$(gh repo view --json nameWithOwner -q .nameWithOwner 2>/dev/null || true)
if [ -z "$REPO" ]; then
  origin_url=$(git -C "$PROJECT_ROOT" remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || true)
  # Strip a trailing ".git" with parameter expansion, not the ERE (vstack#476).
  REPO=$(echo "$origin_url" | sed -nE 's#^.*github\.com[:/]+([^/]+/[^/]+)$#\1#p')
  REPO="${REPO%.git}"
fi
if [ -z "$REPO" ]; then
  emit_error "could not resolve GitHub repo (gh repo view and git remote both failed)"
  echo "Could not resolve GitHub repo (gh repo view and git remote both failed)" >&2
  exit 1
fi

OWNER="${REPO%%/*}"
REPO_NAME="${REPO#*/}"

THREADS_QUERY='query($owner: String!, $repo: String!, $number: Int!, $cursor: String) {
  repository(owner: $owner, name: $repo) {
    pullRequest(number: $number) {
      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $cursor) {
        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
        nodes { isResolved }
      }
    }
  }
}'

# Count unresolved review threads, following reviewThreads pagination past the
# first 100. Prints the total; returns nonzero when a page query fails.
count_unresolved_threads() {
  local cursor="" total=0 threads_json threads_status page_unresolved has_next
  while true; do
    threads_status=0
    set +e
    if [ -n "$cursor" ]; then
      threads_json=$(gh api graphql \
        -f query="$THREADS_QUERY" \
        -F owner="$OWNER" -F repo="$REPO_NAME" -F number="$PR_NUM" \
        -F cursor="$cursor" 2>"$GH_ERR_FILE")
    else
      threads_json=$(gh api graphql \
        -f query="$THREADS_QUERY" \
        -F owner="$OWNER" -F repo="$REPO_NAME" -F number="$PR_NUM" 2>"$GH_ERR_FILE")
    fi
    threads_status=$?
    set -e
    if [ "$threads_status" -ne 0 ] || ! jq -e 'type == "object"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$threads_json"; then
      return 1
    fi
    # `gh api graphql` returns the response envelope; tolerate a pre-stripped
    # object too so stubs and future gh versions both parse.
    page_unresolved=$(jq '(.data // .)
      | [.repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.nodes[]? | select(.isResolved == false)]
      | length' <<<"$threads_json")
    total=$((total + page_unresolved))
    has_next=$(jq -r '(.data // .)
      | .repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.pageInfo.hasNextPage // false' <<<"$threads_json")
    [ "$has_next" = "true" ] || break
    cursor=$(jq -r '(.data // .)
      | .repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.pageInfo.endCursor // ""' <<<"$threads_json")
    [ -n "$cursor" ] || break
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$total"
  return 0
}

# Classify the failed gh call whose stderr sits in GH_ERR_FILE (vstack#748).
# Transient — retryable inside the wait budget — means the service or
# transport hiccuped: HTTP 5xx, HTTP 429 rate limits, timeouts,
# connection/DNS/TLS failures, or a
# timeout(1)-style exit 124. Anything else (HTTP 404, 401/403, malformed
# arguments, unparseable success bodies) is terminal, exactly as before.
gh_failure_is_transient() {
  local gh_status="$1" err_text
  [ "$gh_status" -eq 124 ] && return 0
  err_text="$(cat "$GH_ERR_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  case "$err_text" in
    *"HTTP 5"*) return 0 ;;
    *[Tt]imeout*|*"timed out"*|*"context deadline exceeded"*) return 0 ;;
    *"connection refused"*|*"connection reset"*|*"network is unreachable"*) return 0 ;;
    *"no such host"*|*"could not resolve"*|*[Tt]"emporary failure"*) return 0 ;;
    *"unexpected EOF"*|*"TLS handshake"*) return 0 ;;
    *"HTTP 429"*|*"rate limit"*|*"abuse detection"*) return 0 ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

# Absorb one transient GitHub failure: count and log it, then sleep the
# backed-off interval so the caller can `continue` the poll loop. When the
# max_wait budget is already spent, the failure becomes terminal instead —
# same error message and exit code as the pre-#748 immediate path, plus the
# transient_api_errors count in the JSON.
transient_retry() {
  local what="$1" detail
  detail="$(head -c 200 "$GH_ERR_FILE" 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' ')"
  transient_api_errors=$((transient_api_errors + 1))
  echo "approval-wait: transient GitHub error #$transient_api_errors: $what${detail:+ ($detail)}" >&2
  update_elapsed
  if [ "$elapsed" -ge "$MAX_WAIT" ]; then
    emit_error "$what"
    echo "$what" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  echo "approval-wait: retrying in ${retry_interval}s" >&2
  sleep "$retry_interval"
  retry_interval=$((retry_interval * 2))
  if [ "$retry_interval" -gt "$RETRY_INTERVAL_CAP" ]; then
    retry_interval="$RETRY_INTERVAL_CAP"
  fi
}

# Post the nudge for the current head. With PR_REVIEW_NUDGE set, the nudge is
# that comment body verbatim — the reviewer trigger is user configuration, no
# bot names are baked in. With it empty, fall back to a GitHub-native
# re-review request to the PR's requested and past reviewers; with nobody to
# re-request, keep waiting silently. Failures warn and still count as nudged
# so a broken nudge path can never flood the PR.
post_nudge() {
  local requests_json all_logins payload
  if [ -n "$NUDGE_BODY" ]; then
    if ! gh pr comment "$PR_NUM" --repo "$REPO" --body "$NUDGE_BODY" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      echo "approval-wait: nudge comment failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO" >&2
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  requests_json=$(gh pr view "$PR_NUM" --repo "$REPO" --json reviewRequests 2>/dev/null || printf '{}')
  all_logins=$(printf '%s\n%s\n' \
    "$(jq -r '[.reviewRequests[]? | .login // empty] | .[]' <<<"$requests_json" 2>/dev/null)" \
    "$last_reviewer_logins")
  payload=$(jq -cn --arg logins "$all_logins" \
    '{reviewers: ($logins | split("\n") | map(select(. != "")) | unique)}')
  if [ "$(jq '.reviewers | length' <<<"$payload")" -eq 0 ]; then
    return 0
  fi
  if ! gh api -X POST "repos/$REPO/pulls/$PR_NUM/requested_reviewers" --input - >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$payload"; then
    echo "approval-wait: re-review request failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO" >&2
  fi
}

# Attest a reviewer-outage on the proceeded head (PR_REVIEW_OUTAGE_CONTEXT).
# Called only from the proceed path, so it inherits that path's guarantee of
# genuine reviewer silence. Posts the configured context as `success` on the
# exact head so a repo whose CI gate reads it can turn its required check green
# and its status re-fire bridge can re-run the gate; head-pinned, so a later
# force-push (a new head with no marker) never rides a stale attestation. A
# failure warns but never blocks the proceed verdict — the orch-side degrade
# stands regardless; the marker is best-effort plumbing for repo-side CI.
#
# REVIEW MODE ONLY: the genuine-silence bound requires "no engagement of any
# kind", but only review mode queries PR_REVIEW_CHECK check-runs/statuses into
# last_reviews_present — approval mode counts formal reviews alone, so a
# queued/pending/failed reviewer check could be active there when the marker
# posts, falsely attesting silence. Restricting to review mode is also the
# right scope: the marker exists to unblock a repo-side CI gate that reads
# review-evidence statuses (the review-gated-CI pattern), and a commit status
# cannot satisfy approval mode's native required-approvals branch protection.
post_outage_marker() {
  [ "$MODE" = "review" ] || return 0
  [ -n "$OUTAGE_CONTEXT" ] || return 0
  [ -n "$last_head_sha" ] || return 0
  if gh api -X POST "repos/$REPO/statuses/$last_head_sha" \
      -f state=success \
      -f context="$OUTAGE_CONTEXT" \
      -f "description=orch reviewer-outage: proceeded after ${elapsed}s with no reviewer evidence" \
      >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    last_outage_marker="posted"
  else
    # The proceed verdict stands (the reviewer is genuinely down), but the
    # signal the repo-side CI gate needs was not created — that gate cannot
    # recover on its own. Surface it loudly AND in the JSON (outage_marker:
    # "failed") so the caller can retry the marker or stop, rather than idling
    # ci-wait against a gate that will never turn green.
    last_outage_marker="failed"
    echo "approval-wait: outage-marker status '$OUTAGE_CONTEXT' POST failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO — the repo-side CI gate will NOT recover on its own (check statuses:write); JSON outage_marker=failed." >&2
  fi
}

# Re-read the PR head and confirm it still equals the head the proceed decision
# was made on. head_changed_during_wait only catches changes OBSERVED across
# polls; a push in the final last-poll -> emit window would otherwise let a
# proceed (and its outage marker) land on a now-superseded commit while the
# current-head gate got nothing. On a mismatch — or a failed/empty re-read — the
# caller must treat it as timeout and re-wait on the current head, preserving the
# head-unchanged guarantee. Uses a `-q` head-only query, distinct from the poll
# snapshots, so it is unambiguous.
confirm_head_unchanged() {
  local cur
  cur=$(gh pr view "$PR_NUM" --repo "$REPO" --json headRefOid -q '.headRefOid' 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  [ -n "$cur" ] && [ "$cur" = "$last_head_sha" ]
}

update_elapsed
while true; do
  if [ "$MODE" = "review" ]; then
    # --- head + author snapshot (re-read every poll: a force-push moves the
    # head, and only reviews of the CURRENT head satisfy the gate) ---
    view_status=0
    set +e
    view_json=$(gh pr view "$PR_NUM" --repo "$REPO" --json headRefOid,author 2>"$GH_ERR_FILE")
    view_status=$?
    set -e
    if [ "$view_status" -ne 0 ] || ! jq -e 'type == "object"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$view_json"; then
      if gh_failure_is_transient "$view_status"; then
        transient_retry "gh pr view failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
        continue
      fi
      emit_error "gh pr view failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
      echo "gh pr view failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    last_head_sha=$(jq -r '.headRefOid // ""' <<<"$view_json")
    pr_author=$(jq -r '.author.login // ""' <<<"$view_json")

    # --- submitted reviews snapshot (REST carries commit_id; latestReviews
    # does not, so head pinning needs the pulls/reviews listing) ---
    reviews_status=0
    set +e
    reviews_json=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/pulls/$PR_NUM/reviews" --paginate 2>"$GH_ERR_FILE" | jq -s 'add // []')
    reviews_status=$?
    set -e
    if [ "$reviews_status" -ne 0 ] || ! jq -e 'type == "array"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$reviews_json"; then
      if gh_failure_is_transient "$reviews_status"; then
        transient_retry "review listing failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
        continue
      fi
      emit_error "review listing failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
      echo "review listing failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO" >&2
      exit 1
    fi

    last_review_decision=""
    # A review counts when it is pinned to the current head, submitted (not
    # PENDING), not DISMISSED, and not the PR author's own. Any state counts —
    # COMMENTED and APPROVED alike; an approval is also a review.
    last_reviews_at_head=$(jq --arg head "$last_head_sha" --arg author "$pr_author" \
      '[.[] | select(.commit_id == $head
                     and .state != "DISMISSED" and .state != "PENDING"
                     and (.user.login // "") != $author)] | length' <<<"$reviews_json")
    # A CHANGES_REQUESTED verdict stands until the reviewer re-reviews or it
    # is dismissed — judge each non-author reviewer by their latest submitted
    # review (the REST listing is in submission order).
    last_changes=$(jq --arg author "$pr_author" \
      '[[.[] | select(.state != "DISMISSED" and .state != "PENDING"
                      and (.user.login // "") != $author)]
        | group_by(.user.login) | map(.[-1])
        | .[] | select(.state == "CHANGES_REQUESTED")] | length' <<<"$reviews_json")
    last_approvals=$(jq --arg author "$pr_author" \
      '[[.[] | select(.state != "DISMISSED" and .state != "PENDING"
                      and (.user.login // "") != $author)]
        | group_by(.user.login) | map(.[-1])
        | .[] | select(.state == "APPROVED")] | length' <<<"$reviews_json")
    last_reviewer_logins=$(jq -r --arg author "$pr_author" \
      '[.[] | select((.user.login // "") != $author) | .user.login // empty]
        | unique | .[]' <<<"$reviews_json")

    # --- trusted review-check evidence (vstack#654, vstack#681): consulted
    # only when no review object is pinned to the current head. Both queries
    # target the CURRENT head, so a success on a superseded commit can never
    # satisfy the gate. Check-runs first (when the bot re-analyzed the same
    # head, the newest run of the configured name wins), then — only when no
    # check-run matched, so bots that use check-runs cost no extra API call —
    # the head's combined commit status, for review bots that publish
    # statuses instead of check-runs. Matched by NAME/context (see the
    # SECURITY note at REVIEW_CHECK_NAME); app.slug / creator.login are
    # recorded for the text output, never filtered on. ---
    check_at_head=0
    last_check_surface=""
    last_check_app=""
    last_check_creator=""
    # Reviewer engagement for the proceed-on-timeout guard (review mode): a
    # named check/status present in ANY state means the reviewer engaged this
    # head, so proceed must not treat it as silence. Reset per poll; set below
    # when a check-run or status of the configured name is found. (A review
    # object at head exits as "reviewed" before the deadline, so only the
    # check/status surface can reach the proceed branch here.)
    last_reviews_present=0
    if [ -n "$REVIEW_CHECK_NAME" ] && [ "$last_reviews_at_head" -eq 0 ] \
      && [ -n "$last_head_sha" ]; then
      checks_status=0
      set +e
      checks_json=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/commits/$last_head_sha/check-runs" --paginate 2>"$GH_ERR_FILE" \
        | jq -s '[.[].check_runs[]?]')
      checks_status=$?
      set -e
      if [ "$checks_status" -ne 0 ] || ! jq -e 'type == "array"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$checks_json"; then
        if gh_failure_is_transient "$checks_status"; then
          transient_retry "check-run listing failed for commit $last_head_sha in $REPO"
          continue
        fi
        emit_error "check-run listing failed for commit $last_head_sha in $REPO"
        echo "check-run listing failed for commit $last_head_sha in $REPO" >&2
        exit 1
      fi
      named_check=$(jq -c --arg name "$REVIEW_CHECK_NAME" \
        '[.[] | select(.name == $name)] | sort_by(.started_at // "") | last // {}' <<<"$checks_json")
      last_check_app=$(jq -r '.app.slug // ""' <<<"$named_check")
      # Presence in ANY conclusion (queued/in-progress/failure/success) is
      # reviewer engagement — tracked apart from the success-only gate evidence
      # so proceed-on-timeout cannot fire past an active or failed check.
      if [ -n "$(jq -r '.name // ""' <<<"$named_check")" ]; then
        last_reviews_present=1
      fi
      if [ "$(jq -r '.conclusion // ""' <<<"$named_check")" = "success" ]; then
        check_at_head=1
        last_check_surface="check_run"
      fi
      if [ "$check_at_head" -eq 0 ]; then
        # The combined status reports the latest status per context; the
        # newest-wins sort is a guard for paginated or duplicated contexts.
        # Only state "success" is evidence — pending/failure/error keep
        # waiting, same as a non-success check-run conclusion.
        statuses_status=0
        set +e
        statuses_json=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/commits/$last_head_sha/status" --paginate 2>"$GH_ERR_FILE" \
          | jq -s '[.[].statuses[]?]')
        statuses_status=$?
        set -e
        if [ "$statuses_status" -ne 0 ] || ! jq -e 'type == "array"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$statuses_json"; then
          if gh_failure_is_transient "$statuses_status"; then
            transient_retry "commit-status listing failed for commit $last_head_sha in $REPO"
            continue
          fi
          emit_error "commit-status listing failed for commit $last_head_sha in $REPO"
          echo "commit-status listing failed for commit $last_head_sha in $REPO" >&2
          exit 1
        fi
        named_status=$(jq -c --arg name "$REVIEW_CHECK_NAME" \
          '[.[] | select(.context == $name)] | sort_by(.updated_at // "") | last // {}' <<<"$statuses_json")
        # A named status in ANY state (pending/failure/error/success) is
        # likewise reviewer engagement — track presence apart from success.
        if [ -n "$(jq -r '.context // ""' <<<"$named_status")" ]; then
          last_reviews_present=1
        fi
        if [ "$(jq -r '.state // ""' <<<"$named_status")" = "success" ]; then
          check_at_head=1
          last_check_surface="status"
          last_check_creator=$(jq -r '.creator.login // ""' <<<"$named_status")
        fi
      fi
    fi
  else
    # --- review verdict snapshot (headRefOid rides along for the nudge's
    # once-per-head bookkeeping) ---
    view_status=0
    set +e
    view_json=$(gh pr view "$PR_NUM" --repo "$REPO" --json reviewDecision,latestReviews,headRefOid,author 2>"$GH_ERR_FILE")
    view_status=$?
    set -e
    if [ "$view_status" -ne 0 ] || ! jq -e 'type == "object"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$view_json"; then
      if gh_failure_is_transient "$view_status"; then
        transient_retry "gh pr view failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
        continue
      fi
      emit_error "gh pr view failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
      echo "gh pr view failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO" >&2
      exit 1
    fi

    last_review_decision=$(jq -r '.reviewDecision // ""' <<<"$view_json")
    last_head_sha=$(jq -r '.headRefOid // ""' <<<"$view_json")
    pr_author=$(jq -r '.author.login // ""' <<<"$view_json")
    # latestReviews already holds the latest review per reviewer; count formal
    # verdict states only. COMMENTED/DISMISSED reviews neither approve nor block.
    last_approvals=$(jq '[.latestReviews[]? | select(.state == "APPROVED")] | length' <<<"$view_json")
    last_changes=$(jq '[.latestReviews[]? | select(.state == "CHANGES_REQUESTED")] | length' <<<"$view_json")
    # Any non-author reviewer whose latest review is a formal state (APPROVED,
    # CHANGES_REQUESTED, or COMMENTED — DISMISSED/PENDING excluded) is a reviewer
    # that ENGAGED. The proceed-on-timeout degrade must not fire past one: a
    # COMMENTED review means the reviewer is present, not silent, so proceeding
    # would bypass a live gate rather than route around a dead reviewer.
    last_reviews_present=$(jq --arg author "$pr_author" \
      '[.latestReviews[]? | select((.author.login // "") != $author
        and (.state == "APPROVED" or .state == "CHANGES_REQUESTED" or .state == "COMMENTED"))] | length' <<<"$view_json")
    last_reviewer_logins=$(jq -r '[.latestReviews[]? | .author.login // empty]
      | unique | .[]' <<<"$view_json")
  fi

  # --- unresolved review threads snapshot ---
  threads_status=0
  set +e
  unresolved_total=$(count_unresolved_threads)
  threads_status=$?
  set -e
  if [ "$threads_status" -ne 0 ]; then
    if gh_failure_is_transient "$threads_status"; then
      transient_retry "review thread query failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
      continue
    fi
    emit_error "review thread query failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO"
    echo "review thread query failed for PR #$PR_NUM in $REPO" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  last_unresolved="$unresolved_total"

  # --- route ---
  if [ "$MODE" = "review" ]; then
    if [ "$last_changes" -gt 0 ]; then
      emit_result "changes_requested"
      exit 1
    fi
    review_evidence=""
    if [ "$last_reviews_at_head" -gt 0 ]; then
      review_evidence="review"
    elif [ "$check_at_head" -eq 1 ]; then
      review_evidence="check"
    fi
    if [ -n "$review_evidence" ] && [ "$last_unresolved" -eq 0 ]; then
      last_review_evidence="$review_evidence"
      emit_result "reviewed"
      exit 0
    fi
    if [ "$last_unresolved" -gt 0 ]; then
      emit_result "comments"
      exit 1
    fi
  else
    if [ "$last_review_decision" = "APPROVED" ]; then
      emit_result "approved"
      exit 0
    fi
    if [ "$last_review_decision" = "CHANGES_REQUESTED" ] || [ "$last_changes" -gt 0 ]; then
      emit_result "changes_requested"
      exit 1
    fi
    if [ -z "$last_review_decision" ] && [ "$last_approvals" -gt 0 ]; then
      emit_result "approved"
      exit 0
    fi
    if [ "$last_unresolved" -gt 0 ]; then
      emit_result "comments"
      exit 1
    fi
  fi

  # --- nudge when the gate signal is silent past the nudge window ---
  if [ "$last_head_sha" != "$prev_head_sha" ]; then
    # A new head restarts the nudge clock; the first observed head keeps the
    # wait-start clock. A change after the first observation also disqualifies
    # the proceed-on-timeout degrade for the rest of this wait (see the deadline
    # branch): the reviewer has not had a fair window on the new commit.
    if [ -n "$prev_head_sha" ]; then
      nudge_clock="$(date +%s)"
      head_changed_during_wait=true
    fi
    prev_head_sha="$last_head_sha"
  fi
  nudge_now="$(date +%s)"
  if [ "$NUDGE_SECS" -gt 0 ] \
    && [ $((nudge_now - nudge_clock)) -ge "$NUDGE_SECS" ] \
    && [ "$last_nudged_sha" != "$last_head_sha" ]; then
    post_nudge
    last_nudged_sha="$last_head_sha"
  fi

  update_elapsed
  if [ "$elapsed" -ge "$MAX_WAIT" ]; then
    break
  fi
  # Reaching here means every query in this poll succeeded — end any
  # transient-failure backoff streak (vstack#748).
  retry_interval="$POLL_INTERVAL"
  sleep "$POLL_INTERVAL"
  update_elapsed
done

# Deadline reached with no verdict. Default ("block"): report timeout (exit 1),
# never a silent success. With PR_REVIEW_ON_TIMEOUT=proceed, degrade to
# "proceeded" (exit 0) so a credit-exhausted reviewer that posted nothing does
# not block the fleet — but ONLY on genuine reviewer silence:
#   - zero unresolved threads (an open thread already exited as "comments"),
#   - no reviewer evidence (last_reviews_present == 0). In review mode that
#     means no named trusted check/status is present at head in ANY state — a
#     pending or failed reviewer check is engagement, not silence (a review
#     object at head, or a success check, exits as "reviewed" before the
#     deadline). In approval mode it means no non-author review (COMMENTED
#     included) exists — a reviewer that engaged but did not approve is not
#     "down", so it must timeout, not proceed. And
#   - the head did not change during this wait. elapsed is measured from
#     START_TIME, so a force-push near MAX_WAIT would otherwise proceed seconds
#     after the new commit appeared, before the reviewer could re-review it;
#     fall back to timeout so the caller re-waits on the now-stable head.
# The caller records "proceeded" as a reviewer-down override; CI and the
# comment-hygiene gate still apply.
if [ "$ON_TIMEOUT" = "proceed" ] && [ "$last_unresolved" -eq 0 ] \
  && [ "$last_reviews_present" -eq 0 ] && [ "$head_changed_during_wait" = false ] \
  && confirm_head_unchanged; then
  # The head is confirmed unchanged through to this decision. Attest the outage
  # on it BEFORE emitting, so a repo-side CI gate that reads
  # PR_REVIEW_OUTAGE_CONTEXT already has the signal by the time the caller moves
  # on to verify CI. No-op when the context is unset (orch-side-only).
  post_outage_marker
  emit_result "proceeded"
  exit 0
fi
# Either not a proceed, or the head moved in the final window (confirm failed):
# report timeout so the caller re-waits on the current head. No marker is posted.
emit_result "timeout"
exit 1
